data: {"type":"result","result":{"summary":{"overview":"Apollo 11 in July 1969 first landed humans on the Moon, the culmination of the Cold War Space Race between the United States and the Soviet Union.","sections":[{"heading":"Apollo 11 Mission","points":[{"text":"Apollo 11 launched July 16, 1969, carrying Armstrong, Collins, and Aldrin toward the first crewed Moon landing.","points":[],"chunks":[1]},{"text":"Armstrong and Aldrin landed the Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 while Collins orbited aboard Columbia.","points":["1.5"],"chunks":[1]},{"text":"Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, and Aldrin followed nineteen minutes later.","points":["2.1"],"chunks":[2]},{"text":"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, and the crew splashed down July 24.","points":["3.2"],"chunks":[2,3]}]},{"heading":"Sputnik and the Space Race","points":[{"text":"The Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1 on October 4, 1957, fueling American fears about Soviet technological and military capabilities.","points":["4.1"],"chunks":[4]},{"text":"Sputnik ignited the Space Race, and Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury.","points":["4.3","4.4"],"chunks":[4]},{"text":"Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space on April 12, 1961, and Alan Shepard followed with a suborbital flight.","points":["4.5"],"chunks":[4]}]},{"heading":"Kennedy's Lunar Commitment","points":[{"text":"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should land a man on the Moon before the 1960s ended.","points":["5.3","2.5"],"chunks":[5]},{"text":"Kennedy chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position.","points":["5.1"],"chunks":[5]},{"text":"At Rice University in September 1962, Kennedy said outer space's conquest deserves the best of all mankind.","points":["6.3"],"chunks":[6]},{"text":"Khrushchev declined Kennedy's 1961 proposal for a joint US-Soviet Moon landing.","points":[],"chunks":[7]}]},{"heading":"Mission Architecture Decisions","points":[{"text":"Engineers chose lunar orbit rendezvous over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous as a crucial early decision.","points":["7.6"],"chunks":[7]},{"text":"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced that lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for Apollo.","points":["8.1"],"chunks":[8]},{"text":"The design let a single Saturn V rocket, then under development, launch the Apollo spacecraft.","points":["8.5"],"chunks":[8]}]},{"heading":"Testing Before the Landing","points":[{"text":"The Apollo 1 fire in January 1967 killed three astronauts and abruptly halted Project Apollo.","points":[],"chunks":[2,9]},{"text":"Apollos 7 through 10 tested the command and lunar modules in Earth and lunar orbit before Apollo 11.","points":[],"chunks":[9]}]},{"heading":"Also central in the text","points":[{"text":"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and was NASA's fifth crewed Apollo mission.","points":["1.1"],"chunks":[1]},{"text":"The crew was Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin, all on their second and final spaceflight.","points":["1.2"],"chunks":[1]},{"text":"The crew rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24.","points":["2.3"],"chunks":[2]},{"text":"Apollo 11 was the culmination of the Space Race, a Cold War competition between the United States and the Soviet Union.","points":["2.4"],"chunks":[2]},{"text":"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee and abruptly halted Project Apollo.","points":["9.1"],"chunks":[9]}]}]},"coverage":{"covered":[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9],"missing":[],"noPoints":[],"citedPoints":["1.1","1.2","1.5","2.1","2.3","2.4","2.5","3.2","4.1","4.3","4.4","4.5","5.1","5.3","6.3","7.6","8.1","8.5","9.1"],"uncitedPoints":["1.3","1.4","2.2","3.1","3.3","3.4","3.5","4.2","5.2","5.4","5.5","6.1","6.2","6.4","6.5","7.1","7.2","7.3","7.4","7.5","8.2","8.3","8.4","9.2","9.3","9.4","9.5"],"unchecked":[]},"entities":["Apollo 11","NASA","Apollo program","Neil Armstrong","Michael Collins","Edwin \"Buzz\" Aldrin","Saturn V","Kennedy Space Center","Command Module (CM)","Service Module (SM)","Lunar Module (LM)","Eagle","Armstrong","Aldrin","Collins","Richard Nixon","Space Race","United States","Soviet Union","John F. Kennedy","Apollo 1","Mercury","Gemini","Luna 15","Buzz Aldrin","Columbia","National Air and Space Museum","Presidential Medal of Freedom","Sputnik 1","Dwight D. Eisenhower","National Aeronautics and Space Administration (NASA)","Project Mercury","Yuri Gagarin","Alan Shepard","Kennedy","Eisenhower","United States Congress","Moon","Earth","May 25, 1961","Rice University","Houston, Texas","Texas","Atlantic","mankind","Norbert Wiener","Massachusetts Institute of Technology","Project Apollo","Nikita Khrushchev","United Nations General Assembly","lunar orbit rendezvous","direct ascent","Earth orbit rendezvous","James Webb","Apollo spacecraft","Project Gemini","Interplanetary Monitoring Platform (IMP)","Apollo Guidance Computer (AGC)","Gus Grissom","Ed White","Roger B. Chaffee","Apollo 7","Apollo 8","Apollo 9","Apollo 10"],"retries":0,"chunkCount":9,"findingCount":9,"keyPoints":[{"chunk":1,"points":[{"id":"1.1","text":"Apollo 11 (July 16–24, 1969) was the American spaceflight that first landed humans on the Moon and was NASA's fifth crewed Apollo mission."},{"id":"1.2","text":"The crew was Commander Neil Armstrong, Command Module Pilot Michael Collins, and Lunar Module Pilot Edwin 'Buzz' Aldrin, all on their second and final spaceflight."},{"id":"1.3","text":"The Saturn V launched the Apollo spacecraft from Kennedy Space Center in Florida on July 16, 1969 at 13:32 UTC."},{"id":"1.4","text":"The command module housed the three astronauts and was the only part to return to Earth; the service module provided propulsion, electrical power, oxygen, and water."},{"id":"1.5","text":"After a three-day transit, Armstrong and Aldrin landed the LM Eagle in the Sea of Tranquility on July 20 at 20:17 UTC while Collins remained in lunar orbit aboard Columbia."}]},{"chunk":2,"points":[{"id":"2.1","text":"Armstrong walked on the Moon about six hours after landing, with Aldrin following nineteen minutes later."},{"id":"2.2","text":"The two spent about two and a half hours on the surface, planting a flag, speaking with President Nixon, deploying instruments, and collecting 21.5 kg of lunar material."},{"id":"2.3","text":"The crew rejoined Collins in lunar orbit and returned to Earth, splashing down in the Pacific Ocean on July 24."},{"id":"2.4","text":"Apollo 11 was the culmination of the Space Race, a Cold War competition between the United States and the Soviet Union."},{"id":"2.5","text":"President John F. Kennedy set the goal in May 1961 of landing a man on the Moon before the decade ended; the program overcame the fatal Apollo 1 fire in January 1967."}]},{"chunk":3,"points":[{"id":"3.1","text":"Despite early Soviet leads in spaceflight, the Soviet Union could not match the Saturn V rocket, and its uncrewed probe Luna 15 crashed on the Moon on July 21."},{"id":"3.2","text":"Armstrong's moonwalk was broadcast live to an estimated 600 million viewers, roughly one-fifth of the world's population."},{"id":"3.3","text":"Stepping onto the lunar surface, Armstrong declared: \"That's one small step for [a] man, one giant leap for mankind.\""},{"id":"3.4","text":"Lunar samples led to the identification of three previously unrecognized minerals, and surface instruments kept returning data for years."},{"id":"3.5","text":"The crew received ticker-tape parades, a world goodwill tour, and the Presidential Medal of Freedom; Columbia is preserved at the National Air and Space Museum."}]},{"chunk":4,"points":[{"id":"4.1","text":"On October 4, 1957, the Soviet Union launched Sputnik 1, the first artificial satellite, fueling fears about Soviet technological and military capabilities."},{"id":"4.2","text":"Sputnik's success suggested the USSR could potentially deliver nuclear weapons over intercontinental distances, challenging American claims of superiority."},{"id":"4.3","text":"Sputnik sparked the Sputnik crisis and ignited the Space Race between the two superpowers."},{"id":"4.4","text":"Eisenhower responded by creating NASA and initiating Project Mercury, which aimed to place a man into Earth orbit."},{"id":"4.5","text":"On April 12, 1961, cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space and first to orbit the Earth; Alan Shepard followed on May 5, 1961 with a suborbital flight."}]},{"chunk":5,"points":[{"id":"5.1","text":"Kennedy, Eisenhower's successor, chose a challenge beyond existing rocket capabilities so both countries would start from an equal position."},{"id":"5.2","text":"A crewed Moon mission was presented as the challenge that would serve this purpose."},{"id":"5.3","text":"On May 25, 1961, Kennedy told Congress the nation should commit to landing a man on the Moon and returning him safely before the 1960s end."},{"id":"5.4","text":"Kennedy said no single space project would be more impressive or important for long-range space exploration, and none more difficult or expensive."},{"id":"5.5","text":"Kennedy proposed accelerating lunar spacecraft development, developing alternate liquid and solid fuel boosters, and funding other engine development and unmanned explorations."}]},{"chunk":6,"points":[{"id":"6.1","text":"Kennedy said that going to the Moon would be an entire nation's effort, since all Americans must work to put one man there."},{"id":"6.2","text":"On September 12, 1962, Kennedy gave a speech to about 40,000 people at Rice University's football stadium in Houston, Texas."},{"id":"6.3","text":"Kennedy said outer space had no strife, prejudice, or national conflict yet, and its conquest deserves the best of all mankind."},{"id":"6.4","text":"Kennedy asked why the Moon was chosen as the goal, comparing it to climbing mountains, flying the Atlantic, and Rice playing Texas."},{"id":"6.5","text":"The chunk's refrain 'We choose to go to the Moon' is widely quoted from the middle portion of the Rice speech."}]},{"chunk":7,"points":[{"id":"7.1","text":"Kennedy said the US chose to go to the Moon this decade because the challenge is hard, to organize and measure the best energies and skills."},{"id":"7.2","text":"The proposed Moon program faced widespread opposition; mathematician Norbert Wiener at MIT dubbed it a \"moondoggle.\""},{"id":"7.3","text":"The Moon landing effort already had the name Project Apollo when it was discussed."},{"id":"7.4","text":"Kennedy proposed a joint US-Soviet Moon landing to Premier Nikita Khrushchev in June 1961, but Khrushchev declined."},{"id":"7.5","text":"Kennedy proposed a joint Moon expedition again at the UN General Assembly on September 20, 1963; the idea was abandoned after his death."},{"id":"7.6","text":"An early crucial decision was choosing lunar orbit rendezvous over direct ascent and Earth orbit rendezvous."}]},{"chunk":8,"points":[{"id":"8.1","text":"In July 1962, NASA head James Webb announced lunar orbit rendezvous would be used for Apollo."},{"id":"8.2","text":"The Apollo spacecraft had three major parts: the command module, service module, and lunar module."},{"id":"8.3","text":"The command module had a cabin for three astronauts and was the only part that returned to Earth."},{"id":"8.4","text":"The lunar module had a descent stage for landing on the Moon and an ascent stage to return astronauts to lunar orbit."},{"id":"8.5","text":"This design meant Apollo could be launched by a single Saturn V rocket then under development; Apollo relied on semiconductor advances like MOSFETs and silicon IC chips."}]},{"chunk":9,"points":[{"id":"9.1","text":"The Apollo 1 fire on January 27, 1967 killed astronauts Gus Grissom, Ed White, and Roger B. Chaffee and abruptly halted Project Apollo."},{"id":"9.2","text":"In October 1968, Apollo 7 evaluated the command module in Earth orbit."},{"id":"9.3","text":"In December 1968, Apollo 8 tested the command module in lunar orbit."},{"id":"9.4","text":"In March 1969, Apollo 9 tested the lunar module in Earth orbit; in May, Apollo 10 conducted a lunar orbit 'dress rehearsal'."},{"id":"9.5","text":"By July 1969, all was ready for Apollo 11 to make the final step onto the Moon."}]}],"reviewFlags":["1.1","1.2","2.3","2.4","9.1"],"review":"done","notice":null,"retryOutcome":"none","metrics":{"totalMs":15394,"totalTokens":24983,"totalCost":0.0056663,"priceNote":null,"cheapCost":0.00406,"cheapCalls":10,"synthesisCost":0.0016063,"unreportedCalls":0}}}

